  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 351 AM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\n\nActive weather continues on Sunday through Monday as a shortwave \ntrough pushes off the Rockies and an upper level low deepens over \nNebraska. This system will bring the potential of widespread \nsignificant rainfall to the area. While QPF amounts are still a \nlittle uncertain, confidence is high that most locations will see at \nleast a half inch of rainfall. Some locations across north central \nNebraska could even see over an inch under some of the heavier \nshowers or thunderstorms. While these rainfall totals are \nsignificant, the longer duration of this system should keep any \nflooding concerns at bay. Still, will be monitoring the rainfall \ntotals and flooding potential closely as the event unfolds. \n\nPrecipitation will begin to push out of the area Monday night, \ncompletely exiting the area by Tuesday morning. A brief dry period \nwill push in for mid-week as weak upper level ridging and surface \nhigh pressure builds into the region. A return to some scattered \nshowers is expected on Wednesday, however, confidence in shower \ndevelopment is very low. Will continue to monitor this mid-week \nshowers over the next few days. \n\nFor temperatures, continued rain and clouds as well as cold air \nadvection into the region will keep temperatures cooler than normal \nSunday and Monday. Highs will only rise into the 50s both day, with \nsome locations across the Pine Ridge not rising out of the 40s. \nWarmer weather arrives Tuesday as ridging builds back over Nebraska. \nA return to warm air advection and sunshine will allow temperatures \nto steadily rise back into the low to mid 60s Tuesday and Wednesday.",
